Yossi Matias, Vice President & Head of Google Research, explores how AI is beginning to reshape biology, infrastructure, manufacturing, and science, and why its greatest impact may come when it intersects with other fields.&nbsp;

Step inside the newsroom with our MIT Technology Review editors for sharp analysis and unpublished insights from the team that researches and reports on emerging tech daily.&nbsp;

There’s much more to discover &nbsp;

These are just&nbsp;two&nbsp;sessions from&nbsp; EmTech&nbsp;Future 2026 .

Across three days,&nbsp;EmTech&nbsp;Future explored some of the most important questions shaping technology right now: how AI is&nbsp;impacting industries, where quantum is heading, what new energy systems could make possible, how robotics are evolving, and what&nbsp;challenges and&nbsp;opportunities&nbsp;arise&nbsp;when these technologies start to converge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Hear the ideas, insights, and perspectives you missed at&nbsp;EmTech&nbsp;Future&nbsp;2026. &nbsp;

The full&nbsp;program&nbsp;is now available on demand, giving you access to&nbsp;all&nbsp;the conversations and sessions from the event, including:&nbsp;

Qua ntum in context &nbsp;Hartmut Neven,&nbsp;founder and lead at&nbsp;Google Quantum AI, explores&nbsp;how quantum is advancing alongside other frontiers&nbsp;and why its significance may lie not only in what it can do on its own, but in how it begins to interact with the systems around it.&nbsp;

Energy Systems for the&nbsp;Future &nbsp;Evelyn&nbsp;Wang, VP for&nbsp;energy &&nbsp;climate at MIT, explores how energy, computing, infrastructure, and climate technologies are increasingly interconnected, and what these converging systems could mean for the future of industry and society.&nbsp;

Rethinking our relationship with AI &nbsp;–Cory Doctorow, bestselling author and award-winning writer, explores how AI is changing the way we work and think.&nbsp;

